ci of invasive carcinoma are found in the same general vicinity  however  they exhibit three different morphologies  mucinous  tubular  and lobular configurations  The mucinous carcinoma is associated with a mucinous in situ component  The tubular carcinoma has an associated non mucinous in situ component  while the lobular is associated with lobular carcinoma in situ  These findings would argue for three separate primary tumors  The lobular carcinoma component does exhibit rare tubule like formation  and the majority  but not all  of the invasive tumor cells demonstrate membranous staining with e cadherin antibody  suggesting that it could be classified along with the tubular component as a tubulolobular carcinoma  Dr  Koerner has reviewed selected slides and concurs with this interpretation  The invasive nature of the tubular carcinoma is confirmed with stains for myoepithelial cells  calponin  p63  and myosin   BREAST  RIGHT  FINAL SHAVED MEDIAL 12 00 3 00 MARGIN   EXCISION  NO IN SITU OR INVASIVE CARCINOMA IS IDENTIFIED  NOTE  A cytokeratin immunoperoxidase stain helps support that a focus of crushed cells is stromal rather than epithelial  BREAST  RIGHT  FINAL SHAVED MEDIAL 3 00 6 00 MARGIN   EXCISION  NO DEFINITE IN SITU OR INVASIVE CARCINOMA IS IDENTIFIED  BREAST  RIGHT  FINAL SHAVED LATERAL HALF OF CAVITY MARGIN   EXCISION  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  GRADE REMOVED_DATE  ONE MICROSCOPIC FOCUS  SPANNING APPROXIMATELY 0 55CM  LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU  NOTE  The presence of both in situ ductal and lobular carcinoma is confirmed by an immunohistochemical stain for e cadherin  LYMPH NODE  RIGHT AXILLA   SENTINEL    EXCISION  METASTATIC CARCINOMA IN ONE OUT OF ONE LYMPH NODE  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The metastatic tumor glands aggregate to approximately 0 18cm  micrometastasis  and are identified on both the HE and cytokeratin immunoperoxidase stained slides  No extracapsular extension is identified  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PAT